Before starting

We will assume your inventory looks something like:

[webservers]
web1
web2

And you can already connect to these servers using Ansible.

Check connectivity first:

ansible all -i inventory.ini -m ping

You should get:

web1 | SUCCESS => {
    "ping": "pong"
}

web2 | SUCCESS => {
    "ping": "pong"
}

If this works, start the error-handling labs.

=> LAB 1 — register

Objective

We want Ansible to run a command and save its result.

Create:

nano register.yml

Put:

---
- hosts: all
  become: true

  tasks:

    - name: Check Docker version
      ansible.builtin.command: docker --version
      register: docker_result
      ignore_errors: yes

    - name: Display Docker result
      ansible.builtin.debug:
        var: docker_result

Run:

ansible-playbook -i inventory.ini register.yml
What is happening?

First task:

register: docker_result

means:

Run the Docker command and save its complete result inside docker_result.

means:

Show me what Ansible saved.

You may see:

docker_result:
    failed: false
    rc: 0
    stdout: "Docker version 29.7.2..."
    stderr: ""

So remember:

register = SAVE the result
debug    = SHOW the result

=> LAB 2 — ignore_errors
Objective

We want to continue the playbook even if a task fails.

Create:

nano ignore-errors.yml

---
- hosts: all
  become: true

  tasks:

    - name: Try to run a command that does not exist
      ansible.builtin.command: command_that_does_not_exist
      ignore_errors: yes

    - name: This task should still run
      ansible.builtin.debug:
        msg: "Playbook continued after the error"

Run:

ansible-playbook -i inventory.ini ignore-errors.yml

You will see the first task fail.

But then you should see:

TASK [This task should still run]
ok: [web1]

=> What happened?

Normally:

Task fails
   ↓
Play stops for that host

=> With ignore_errors: yes

it becomes:

Task fails
   ↓
Ignore failure
   ↓
Continue
   ↓
Next task runs

For example:

Check whether an optional package exists. If it doesn't exist, continue with the deployment.

But don't blindly use ignore_errors everywhere.

Industry use

You might use this when a failure is expected and non-critical.

If a critical task fails, ignoring it can create problems.

=> LAB 3 — register + failed + rc + stdout + stderr

Now let's combine what you learned earlier.

Create:

nano result-details.yml

---
- hosts: all
  become: true

  tasks:

    - name: Check Docker version
      ansible.builtin.command: docker --version
      register: docker_result
      ignore_errors: yes

    - name: Show whether task failed
      ansible.builtin.debug:
        msg: "Failed status: {{ docker_result.failed }}"

    - name: Show return code
      ansible.builtin.debug:
        msg: "Return code: {{ docker_result.rc }}"

    - name: Show command output
      ansible.builtin.debug:
        msg: "Docker output: {{ docker_result.stdout }}"

Run:

ansible-playbook -i inventory.ini result-details.yml

Now you are accessing individual pieces of the registered result.

For example:

docker_result.failed

means:

Give me the failed value.

And:

docker_result.stdout

means:

Give me the normal output of the command.

=> LAB 4 — failed_when

This is one of the most important concepts for real-world Ansible.

Objective

We want to tell Ansible:

"Don't use only your normal understanding of success/failure. I will define what should count as failure."

Create:

nano failed-when.yml

Use:

---
- hosts: all

  tasks:

    - name: Check current uptime
      ansible.builtin.command: uptime
      register: uptime_result
      failed_when: "'days' in uptime_result.stdout"

Run:

ansible-playbook -i inventory.ini failed-when.yml

Here we are saying:

failed_when: "'days' in uptime_result.stdout"

Meaning:

If the word days appears in the command output, consider the task failed.

This is just for learning the concept.


=> Why would we do this in real life?

Suppose an application health-check command returns:

Application status: FAILED

but the command itself returns:

rc: 0

Ansible might normally think:

rc = 0
     ↓
Success

But you know the application is actually unhealthy.

So you can say:

failed_when: "'FAILED' in result.stdout"

Now:

Command runs
    ↓
Check output
    ↓
"FAILED" found
    ↓
Ansible marks task as failed

That's a very practical use of failed_when.

=> LAB 5 — changed_when

Now let's understand another important concept.

Create:

nano changed-when.yml

Use:

---
- hosts: all

  tasks:

    - name: Check hostname
      ansible.builtin.command: hostname
      register: hostname_result
      changed_when: false

    - name: Display hostname
      ansible.builtin.debug:
        var: hostname_result.stdout

Run:

ansible-playbook -i inventory.ini changed-when.yml

You should see the hostname task reported as:

ok

instead of:

changed
Why?

We only ran:

hostname

We didn't actually change anything on the server.

=> LAB 6 — block

Now we'll group multiple related tasks.

Create:

nano block.yml

Put:

---
- hosts: all
  become: true

  tasks:

    - name: Application setup
      block:

        - name: Create application directory
          ansible.builtin.file:
            path: /opt/myapp
            state: directory

        - name: Create configuration file
          ansible.builtin.file:
            path: /opt/myapp/app.conf
            state: touch

        - name: Display success message
          ansible.builtin.debug:
            msg: "Application setup completed"

Run:

ansible-playbook -i inventory.ini block.yml
What does block do?

It groups related tasks:

Application setup
       |
       ├── Create directory
       ├── Create config
       └── Display message

This becomes especially powerful when combined with:

block
rescue
always

=> LAB 7 — block + rescue

This is one of the most important practical error-handling patterns.

Create:

nano block-rescue.yml

Put:

---
- hosts: all
  become: true

  tasks:

    - name: Application deployment
      block:

        - name: Create application directory
          ansible.builtin.file:
            path: /opt/myapp
            state: directory

        - name: Run a command that will fail
          ansible.builtin.command: command_that_does_not_exist

        - name: This task will not run
          ansible.builtin.debug:
            msg: "This will not execute"

      rescue:

        - name: Handle deployment failure
          ansible.builtin.debug:
            msg: "Application deployment failed. Running recovery actions."

Run:

ansible-playbook -i inventory.ini block-rescue.yml
What happens?

First:

Create directory
       ↓
SUCCESS

Then:

Run invalid command
       ↓
FAIL

Because the task is inside a block, Ansible goes to:

rescue

So:

BLOCK
  |
  ├── Task 1 ✅
  ├── Task 2 ❌
  └── Task 3 ⛔
          ↓
       RESCUE
          ↓
    Recovery task
Important

rescue is not the same as ignore_errors.

=> ignore_errors says:

"Ignore the failure and continue."

=> rescue says:

"The task failed, so now perform these recovery actions."

That's a major difference.

=> LAB 8 — block + rescue + always

Now let's complete the pattern.

Create:

nano block-rescue-always.yml

Put:

---
- hosts: all
  become: true

  tasks:

    - name: Application deployment
      block:

        - name: Create application directory
          ansible.builtin.file:
            path: /opt/myapp
            state: directory

        - name: Simulate deployment failure
          ansible.builtin.command: command_that_does_not_exist

      rescue:

        - name: Handle failure
          ansible.builtin.debug:
            msg: "Deployment failed. Recovery action started."

      always:

        - name: Final message
          ansible.builtin.debug:
            msg: "Deployment process completed."

Run:

ansible-playbook -i inventory.ini block-rescue-always.yml

The flow is:

BLOCK
  |
  ├── Create directory ✅
  |
  └── Invalid command ❌
            |
            ↓
         RESCUE
            |
            └── Recovery message
            |
            ↓
         ALWAYS
            |
            └── Final message

Even though the task failed, always runs.

=> LAB 9 — assert

Now let's validate something before continuing.

Suppose your application requires a particular Linux distribution.

Create:

nano assert.yml

Put:

---
- hosts: all

  tasks:

    - name: Check operating system
      ansible.builtin.assert:
        that:
          - ansible_facts['os_family'] == "Debian"
        success_msg: "This is a Debian-based system"
        fail_msg: "This playbook requires a Debian-based system"

Run:

ansible-playbook -i inventory.ini assert.yml
What is happening?

Ansible checks:

Is OS family Debian?
       |
    ┌──┴──┐
   YES    NO
    |      |
 Continue  Fail

This is useful in production because you don't want to run a Debian-specific task against a RHEL server accidentally.

=> LAB 10 — fail

Now let's intentionally stop when a condition isn't acceptable.

Create:

nano fail.yml

Put:

---
- hosts: all

  tasks:

    - name: Check disk space
      ansible.builtin.shell: df -h / | tail -1
      register: disk_result

    - name: Stop if disk check fails
      ansible.builtin.fail:
        msg: "Stopping deployment because disk space requirement was not met."
      when: false

The when: false is only being used here so you can safely see the structure without actually stopping your lab.

The important concept is:

Check something
     ↓
Condition not acceptable
     ↓
fail
     ↓
Stop with your own message

=> LAB 11 — assert vs fail

These are easy to confuse.

assert

Think:

"Check this condition."

Is condition true?
      ↓
 YES → Continue
 NO  → Fail

fail

Think:

"I have decided that this situation is unacceptable. Stop and show this message."

=> LAB 12 — any_errors_fatal

This is important when working with multiple production servers.

Suppose you have:

web1
web2
web3
web4

You run a critical deployment.

If web1 fails, you may not want the deployment to continue on the other servers.

Create:

nano any-errors-fatal.yml
---
- hosts: all
  any_errors_fatal: true

  tasks:

    - name: Simulate critical failure
      ansible.builtin.command: command_that_does_not_exist

Run:

ansible-playbook -i inventory.ini any-errors-fatal.yml

The important setting is:

any_errors_fatal: true

Meaning:

If an error occurs, treat it as a fatal error for the play rather than allowing the play to continue independently across hosts.

Note:-

Industry example

Imagine you're updating a database schema across several servers.

If one critical server fails halfway through, you may want to stop the operation rather than continue changing other servers.

=> LAB 13 — max_fail_percentage

Now imagine you have:

100 servers

You don't necessarily want to stop because one server failed.

Maybe your requirement is:

If more than 20% of servers fail, stop the deployment.

You can use:

max_fail_percentage: 20

Example:

nano max-failure.yml

Put:

---
- hosts: all
  max_fail_percentage: 20

  tasks:

    - name: Run application health check
      ansible.builtin.command: /opt/myapp/healthcheck.sh

The idea is:

100 servers
   |
   ├── 5 fail  → Continue
   ├── 10 fail → Continue
   └── 21 fail → Stop

=> LAB 14 — Realistic Industry Scenario

Now let's combine the concepts.

Imagine you're deploying an application.

The process is:

1. Check application
2. Stop application
3. Deploy new files
4. Start application
5. Check application health
6. If something fails → recover
7. Always perform cleanup/final action

Create:

nano application-deployment.yml

Put:

---
- hosts: all
  become: true

  tasks:

    - name: Application deployment
      block:

        - name: Check application service
          ansible.builtin.command: systemctl is-active myapp
          register: service_status
          changed_when: false
          failed_when: false

        - name: Stop application
          ansible.builtin.systemd:
            name: myapp
            state: stopped

        - name: Deploy application files
          ansible.builtin.copy:
            src: app/
            dest: /opt/myapp/

        - name: Start application
          ansible.builtin.systemd:
            name: myapp
            state: started

        - name: Check application health
          ansible.builtin.command: /opt/myapp/healthcheck.sh
          register: health_result
          changed_when: false
          failed_when: health_result.rc != 0

      rescue:

        - name: Application deployment failed
          ansible.builtin.debug:
            msg: "Deployment failed. Starting recovery procedure."

        - name: Restart application
          ansible.builtin.systemd:
            name: myapp
            state: started

      always:

        - name: Deployment completed
          ansible.builtin.debug:
            msg: "Deployment process finished."

=> Understand the industry scenario step by step
Step 1 — block
block:

We're saying:

These tasks together represent one application deployment operation.

Step 2 — register
register: service_status

We're saving the service status so we can use the result later.

Step 3 — changed_when: false
changed_when: false

We're only checking the service.

We're not changing anything.

So we tell Ansible:

Don't report this check as changed.

___________
Note:- If we write 
changed_when: true

then Ansible will always mark that task as changed, even if the task only checks something and doesn't actually modify the server.
____________

Step 4 — failed_when: false
failed_when: false

We're saying:

Don't consider this particular check itself a failure.

Why?

Because we're just checking the current status. We may want to handle the status ourselves instead of immediately stopping the play.

_______________
Note:- you change it to:

failed_when: true

then Ansible will always consider that task as FAILED, regardless of whether the command itself actually succeeds.
_______________

Step 5 — Deployment

Then we:

Stop application
      ↓
Copy new files
      ↓
Start application

These are actual changes.

Step 6 — Health check

We run:

/opt/myapp/healthcheck.sh

and save the result:

register: health_result

Then:

failed_when: health_result.rc != 0

means:

If the health-check command returns anything other than 0, consider the deployment failed.

This is a very realistic use of failed_when.

Step 7 — Failure

Suppose:

Health check
     ↓
rc = 1
     ↓
failed_when condition becomes TRUE
     ↓
Task fails

Now Ansible goes to:

rescue

Step 8 — Rescue

The rescue section can attempt recovery:

Deployment failed
       ↓
Restart application
       ↓
Try to recover

This is much better than simply saying:

ignore_errors: yes

because we're actually doing something about the failure.

Step 9 — Always

Finally:

always:

runs regardless of whether deployment succeeded or failed.

So:

SUCCESS → always
FAILURE → rescue → always

=> Final Error Handling Flow

This is the flow I recommend you remember:

                    TASK
                     |
                     ↓
               Run the task
                     |
                     ↓
             Save result?
                     |
                  register
                     |
                     ↓
              Did it fail?
               /          \
             NO            YES
             |              |
             |        Should we ignore?
             |          /        \
             |        YES         NO
             |         |           |
             |  ignore_errors      |
             |                     |
             |                  rescue
             |                     |
             |                     ↓
             |                 Recovery
             |                     |
             └──────────┬──────────┘
                        |
                        ↓
                     always
                        |
                        ↓
                   Next/Finish



